<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :open_source %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:open_source) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">

      <!-- Commemorative hero screen -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'What This Repository Is') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework and the site that documents it live together in one Rails app. It has no database. It compiles the CSS and JavaScript, then renders these pages against them.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Framework development happens against the docs: edit the Sass or JS, watch it rebuild, and check the result on the page it documents. The screen below is a plugin layout built from framework classes, the same building blocks every example here uses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col gap--space-between">
                    <div class="flex flex--col gap--small">
                      <span class="title title--large">TRMNL Framework</span>
                      <span class="description">The ePaper design system is now open source.</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="grid">
                      <div class="row">
                        <div class="item col--span-2">
                          <div class="content">
                            <span class="value">3.3</span>
                            <span class="label">Version</span>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="item col--span-2">
                          <div class="content">
                            <span class="value">MIT</span>
                            <span class="label">Code license</span>
                          </div>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <%= image_tag plugin_image_path("trmnl--render.svg"), class: "image image--adaptive", alt: "TRMNL Logo" %>
                    <span class="title">Open Source</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The fixed hierarchy of Screen, View, Layout, and Title Bar is documented in <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :structure) %>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- The Design System (CSS) -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Design System') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The CSS is a Sass design system under <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/assets/stylesheets/framework/</code>. It renders one layout correctly across very different screens. The SCSS source and what a custom stack can build from it are documented in <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :sass_api) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li><strong>Bit-depth-aware tokens:</strong> a color resolves to dither patterns on 1-bit, palette images on limited color, and solids on full color. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :colors) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :tokens) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>Dither and tile backgrounds:</strong> grayscale and limited-palette fills are generated tiles, applied with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--{shade}</code>. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :background) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>Device screen classes:</strong> <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen--&lt;device&gt;</code> bakes each device's dimensions and palette into the build. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :screen) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>Themes:</strong> opt-in stylesheets repaint a whole screen while preserving device-capability rendering. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- The Runtime (JavaScript) -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Runtime') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The JavaScript runtime lives in <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">app/javascript/</code> and ships as a stamped pair: the readable <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.js</code> and the minified <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.min.js</code> production serves, both carrying the build marker of the released version. It does the render-time work a static stylesheet cannot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li><strong>terminalize:</strong> the layout engine that fills each device's fixed space, handling overflow columns, text clamping, value fitting, and more. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :framework_runtime) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>TRMNLPaint:</strong> reads the live CSS cascade and returns canonical Fill, BorderFill, and TypeSpec objects, so any plugin can resolve framework colors from JavaScript. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>TRMNLCharts:</strong> a Highcharts adapter built on TRMNLPaint, so charts adapt to bit depth, dark mode, and themes with the rest of the screen. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>TRMNLMaps:</strong> a MapLibre GL JS adapter built on TRMNLPaint, so a plotted map over OpenStreetMap vector tiles adapts the same way. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :map) %>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Core mandate -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'CSS Is the Single Source of Truth for Paint') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            One rule shapes the whole project. Every rendering rule exists in CSS first, then <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> mirrors it.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> reads the live cascade and converts it. It never re-implements token mappings, adds contrast heuristics, or invents fallback values in JavaScript.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The same instinct applies to new features. Before adding a pattern, tile, gradient, or one-off variable, check whether the dither and tile system, the border pipeline, or the theme slots already express it. A new bespoke mechanism for one call site is almost always wrong.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The full mandate is in <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">AGENTS.md</code>. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> for how the API reads the cascade.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Release pipeline -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Release Pipeline') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Releases are reproducible and need only this repository. A release regenerates the tokens, compiles the Sass with Dart Sass, processes the CSS and the version-stamped JS runtime through the bundle pipelines, precompresses every artifact as gzip and brotli deterministically, and zips the result.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The published archive under <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">public/css</code> and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">public/js</code> keeps every version, so the docs render each version against the exact bundle it shipped. Releases are maintainer-only.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Third-party origins -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'What This Site Loads from Elsewhere') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework bundles fetch from one host outside their own origin: a map that names no tile source fetches OpenStreetMap's public tiles directly. Pattern images and fonts come from wherever <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.css</code> came from.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The docs site around them loads from four third-party origins, all in the page chrome or in a demo. Each one fails on its own page, so a host that blocks all four still serves every page here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li><strong>Google Fonts</strong> (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fonts.googleapis.com</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fonts.gstatic.com</code>): Inter and EB Garamond for this site, Space Mono for code, and Inter again inside every demo iframe. Blocked, the site falls back to system fonts and the screens keep their self-hosted families. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :font_family) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>unpkg</strong> (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">unpkg.com</code>): the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@trmnl/picker</code> component behind the device selector. Blocked, the picker stays blank.</li>
          <li><strong>trmnl.com</strong>: Highcharts and Chartkick, on the chart page and in the Shopify example. Blocked, those charts render empty. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>.</li>
          <li><strong>jsDelivr</strong> (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">cdn.jsdelivr.net</code>): opentype.js, which reads the glyph tables. Blocked, those tables stay empty. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :font_glyphs) %>.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Mounting the engine in another Rails app brings all four with it. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">docs/ENGINE_INTEGRATION.md</code> repeats this list with the CSP directives each origin needs.
        </p>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Map tiles come from OpenStreetMap's public Shortbread endpoint (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">vector.openstreetmap.org</code>) by default, fetched by the page itself on the free tier, whose usage policy allows light use and forbids a fleet. A plugin names its own source and key, a host injects one per plugin instance, and the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'trmnl'</code> preset names TRMNL's own planet at <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">maps.trmnl.com</code>, which the docs demos use. The engine also serves <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">/framework/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.mvt</code> for a host proxying a source of its own (a pass-through from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Framework.tile_source_url</code>; nothing is stored).
        </p>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">docs/MAPS_GO_LIVE.md</code> is the checklist for a host that renders map plugins for devices. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :map) %>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- License -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'License') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The framework code is released under the MIT license. See the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">LICENSE</code> file in the repository.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The MIT grant covers the code. The bundled fonts are licensed separately: the TRMNL pixel fonts by Heavyweight Digital Type Foundry, Inter, and the Nico fonts ship under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, and BlockKie under CC BY 3.0. Each font bundle download carries the full terms in its README and OFL.txt.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Highcharts is a commercial library the framework does not include. The chart examples load it from trmnl.com, where TRMNL serves it under its own license, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> is only the adapter. A custom stack brings its own charting library and license: see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            MapLibre GL JS is BSD licensed and vendored under <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">vendor/javascript/</code>, served by the engine next to the runtime and mirrored for plugins at <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">trmnl.com/js/maplibre-gl/5.24.0/</code>; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md</code> carries its notice. The map data is &copy; OpenStreetMap contributors under the ODbL, which is why every map keeps its credit visible, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLMaps</code> is only the adapter: see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :map) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Start here -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Start Here') %>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <li>Want to help build it? Read <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :contributing) %>.</li>
            <li>New to the framework? Start with <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :structure) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :colors) %>.</li>
            <li>Adopting the 3.2 and 3.3 features? See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :v3_overview) %>.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
